desc:Riomhdhos input envelope (publishes playing dynamics to the mood tracks)
// Goes FIRST on a live input track (Zoom [1] = guitar, Zoom [2] = voice/second source).
//
// Measures how hard you are playing and publishes it through gmem, which every JSFX in
// the session shares. The layer mixer on each mood track reads it and ducks the drone.
// No MIDI routing, no sends, no track order dependency - just a number in shared memory.
//
//   gmem[0] = input 1 envelope (guitar)     gmem[1] = input 2 envelope (voice)
//   gmem[8] / gmem[9] = peak hold, for metering
//
// AUDIO IS PASSED THROUGH COMPLETELY UNTOUCHED. This only measures. It is safe to leave
// on a live input permanently, and safe to leave enabled when the duck depth is zero.
//
// This is the "reactivity" idea from the original Pd rig, finally wired up: the drone
// breathes with your playing instead of sitting flat underneath it.

options:gmem=riomhdhos

slider1:0<0,7,1>gmem slot (0 = guitar, 1 = second input)
slider2:8<1,200,1>Attack (ms)
slider3:260<20,2000,1>Release (ms)
slider4:-40<-70,-10,1>Sensitivity floor (dB)
slider5:0<0,1,1{off,on}>Show level below
slider6:0<0,1,0.001>Current envelope (read only)

@init
env = 0; pk = 0; disp = 0;

@slider
atk = exp(-1 / (srate * max(0.001, slider2 * 0.001)));
rel = exp(-1 / (srate * max(0.001, slider3 * 0.001)));
floorlin = 10 ^ (slider4 / 20);

@sample
// rectified peak of whichever channels are present
a = abs(spl0); b = abs(spl1);
lvl = max(a, b);

// asymmetric follower: fast up so a pick attack registers, slow down so the drone
// returns gently rather than pumping
lvl > env ? ( env = lvl + (env - lvl) * atk; )
          : ( env = lvl + (env - lvl) * rel; );

// map the useful dynamic range onto 0..1 - below the floor counts as silence
norm = (env - floorlin) / (1 - floorlin);
norm = max(0, min(1, norm));

gmem[slider1] = norm;
pk = max(pk * 0.99999, norm);
gmem[8 + slider1] = pk;

// audio continues untouched - spl0/spl1 are deliberately never written

@block
slider5 ? (
  disp != gmem[slider1] ? ( slider6 = gmem[slider1]; sliderchange(slider6); disp = slider6; );
);
